WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:05.030
Is Joaquin Phoenix a spoiled movie star…
or is he pulling our collective leg once again?

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:08.760
During an appearance on Jimmy Kimmel Live!,
Phoenix was presented with an outtake from

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:13.470
Joker in which he appears to go on a profane
rant. Much awkward joking between Phoenix

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:17.940
and Kimmel then ensued, and we're not sure
exactly what to make of this for so many reasons.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:22.500
Phoenix and Kimmel were engaging in easy banter,
the star appeared engaged and relaxed, and

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.420
everything was going fine until Kimmel appeared
to drop a pretty awkward bombshell. Specifically,

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:32.160
he showed a clip from a supposed "outtake
reel" courtesy of Joker director Todd Phillips

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.609
that didn't exactly show the most flattering
side of Mr. Phoenix. In the clip, Phoenix

00:00:36.609 --> 00:00:41.429
gaunt and partially made up is attempting
to tap into his character, Arthur Fleck, aka

00:00:41.429 --> 00:00:45.840
the Joker. Unfortunately, this proves difficult
thanks in no small part to the "constant whispering"

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.319
of a crew member named Larry.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:52.059
"Like, the constant whispering, just shut
the f--- up, dude. I'm trying to, like, find

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:52.940
something real."

00:00:53.089 --> 00:00:56.800
Although it's not heard, it's implied that
a man named Larry accuses Phoenix of being

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:01.120
a diva by way of referring to him as "Cher,"
which irks the star even more.

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:04.700
“And I know you started the f------ Cher
thing, Larry."

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:09.640
"That's not even an insult. Cher? Really?
Singer, actor, dancer, fashion icon, how is

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:10.280
that a f------ insult?"

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:14.380
Phoenix then declares that he simply can't
carry on, and walks out of the frame.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:18.240
When the interview resumes, Phoenix appears
profoundly embarrassed, adjusting his tie

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:19.900
and appearing to start sweating.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:24.580
"... movies get intense, because you're a
lot of people in a small space, and you're

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:27.340
trying to find something, that was supposed
to be private."

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:31.720
He says that last part somewhat jokingly,
and throughout, the audience is laughing along,

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:33.340
giving him the benefit of the doubt.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:38.180
It's rather disarming and a little weird to
see such a massively talented actor set completely

00:01:38.189 --> 00:01:42.590
off his axis, looking confused and mortified
unless, that is, you're familiar with the

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:47.340
months-long piece of performance art which
Phoenix staged roughly a decade ago. In October

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:51.619
2008, Phoenix made the surprising announcement
that he was quitting acting to focus full-time

00:01:51.619 --> 00:01:56.079
on music rap music, to be exact. He made good
on his promise to become a rapper early the

00:01:56.079 --> 00:02:00.570
following year, with a debut performance in
Las Vegas that was equal parts unintentionally

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:02.400
hilarious and actually painful.

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:07.180
For the remainder of 2009, Phoenix publicly
insisted that he was done with acting while

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:11.620
apparently attempting to barrel forward with
his rap career. Remember that famously bizarre,

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:13.840
awkward appearance on The Late Show with David
Letterman?

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:15.160
"How is that? The beard?"

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:16.800
"In what way?"

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:20.460
"Well, is it comfortable? Is it itchy? Is
it — are you pleased with it?"

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.040
"I'm OK with it, but now you're making me
feel weird about it."

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:27.980
Not long after the interview, word leaked
out that the star had enlisted director Casey

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:32.510
Affleck to direct a documentary following
his transition from movie star to dope MC.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:39.000
"I said I'll never go back to acting and now
I can't I don’t know I’m miserable ? f-------

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.360
music sucks and I didn't even f--- know it…
I'm so stupid."

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:47.400
In 2010, the film titled I'm Still Here was
released, and shortly thereafter, the ruse

00:02:47.409 --> 00:02:52.040
fell apart. It had all been a giant hoax,
one that Phoenix had perpetrated in public

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:56.800
with a straight face for over a year. He returned
to acting in 2012 with a brilliant performance

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:00.879
in Paul Thomas Anderson's The Master, and
recently, he's stated that the whole thing

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:04.320
was a gag between himself and Affleck that
got seriously out of hand.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:08.670
Considering the extreme lengths to which Phoenix
was willing to go to put one over on his fans,

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:13.579
the Jimmy Kimmel Live! bit seems well, a bit
suspect. Why would Phillips blindside his

00:03:13.579 --> 00:03:17.409
star by sending the talk show host such an
embarrassing outtake? Why would Kimmel drop

00:03:17.409 --> 00:03:19.360
it on Phoenix without warning him in advance?

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:23.760
At the end of the day, the I'm Still Here
experiment proved beyond a shadow of a doubt

00:03:23.769 --> 00:03:26.970
that there are no actors quite so willing
to yank the chains of their audience like

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:30.681
Phoenix and it turns out that the profane
outtake screened on Jimmy Kimmel Live! was

00:03:30.681 --> 00:03:35.420
a hoax, as the actor's publicist told Entertainment
Weekly that it was a, quote, "joke outtake."

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:39.840
And that's a good thing, too. Joaquin Phoenix
seems like a pretty well-adjusted guy so we

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:43.219
had our fingers crossed that he wasn't as
big of a jerk as his character Arthur Fleck

00:03:43.219 --> 00:03:47.290
inevitably turns out to be when he assumes
the identity of the Joker in the Clown Prince

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:49.020
of Crime's next big-screen outing.

